course: dictionary
lesson: 11365
title: Abate: when things die down
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Abate is a verb. When a storm, wind, pain or noise abates, it gets weaker or smaller. «The storm abated.» means the storm became less strong, so the danger was passing.
explain: Look for clues around the word. «The rain abated after an hour, and the children ran out to play.» The children went out because the rain was getting less. Ask yourself: what was getting weaker?
explain: In formal writing, abate can also mean to make something less or put an end to it. «The council must abate the noise from the building site.» Here it is a stiff, official way of saying stop or reduce.
fact: Abate means to become less strong or less in amount.
fact: «Abate» is a formal word; in everyday talk we say «die down» or «ease off».
fact: «Unabated» means still as strong as before: «The wind blew unabated.»
fact: The forms are abate, abates, abated and abating: «The pain abated» and «the pain is abating».
fact: To check your meaning, swap in «got weaker» and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «The storm abated by the evening.» What does abated mean here? | Started for the first time | *Got weaker | Turned into snow | Got louder
exercise: «The pain did not abate, so she went to the doctor.» What does this tell you? | *The pain got no weaker | The pain went away at once | The doctor was angry | The pain was a happy feeling
exercise: «The rain fell unabated all night.» What does unabated mean? | *Still as strong as before | Weaker than before | Stopped for a rest | Very cold
test: «The council was told to abate the smoke from the factory.» In this sentence, abate means | *To make it less or stop it | To paint it | To make it bigger | To move it away
test: «The storm showed no signs of abating.» What does this mean? | The storm was over before it began | The storm was getting weaker quickly | The storm had stopped | *The storm was not getting weaker
exam: «The noise from the road did not abate all day.» What does this tell you about the noise? | It stopped at breakfast | It was a happy sound | It went quiet at lunchtime | *It stayed just as loud all day
